## Staged vs Unstaged Context

### What the Agent Sees Based on Git State

| Git State | Agent Sees | Best Practice |
|-----------|-----------|---------------|
| **All staged** | Only staged diff | Stage changes before asking agent to commit |
| **Some staged** | Staged diff only | Stage only what you want the agent to see |
| **Unstaged** | Working tree diff | Agent sees all changes, including uncommitted |
| **No changes** | Nothing | Ask agent to identify what needs attention |

### How Context Flows to the Agent

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Agent is invoked] --> B{Git state?}
    B -->|Staged changes| C[git diff --cached]
    B -->|Unstaged changes| D[git diff]
    B -->|No changes| E[git status]
    
    C --> F[Agent sees staged diff]
    D --> G[Agent sees all changes]
    E --> H[Agent sees file list only]
    
    F --> I[Commit message from staged]
    G --> J[Commit message from all]
    H --> K[Agent asks for context]
```

### Staged-Only Commit Pattern

```bash
# 1. Make changes to a file
vim src/auth/oauth.ts

# 2. Stage only the relevant changes
git add src/auth/oauth.ts

# 3. Open Cursor Composer (Cmd+K)
#    Agent now sees only staged diff

# 4. Ask: "Generate a conventional commit message for these changes"
#    Agent generates: feat(auth): add OAuth2 login flow

# 5. Commit
git commit -m "feat(auth): add OAuth2 login flow"
```

### Multi-Stage Commit Pattern

```bash
# When a file has multiple logical changes

# 1. View file and identify separate changes
vim src/api/server.ts

# 2. Use git add -p to selectively stage
git add -p src/api/server.ts
# Stage only the logging changes first

# 3. Ask agent to generate commit for staged changes
# → fix(api): add request logging middleware

# 4. Continue with git add -p for next change
# → refactor(api): extract route handlers

# 5. Commit each stage separately
```

